# git-LFS tracking
#
# Demo videos and large reference Figma documents can balloon repo size when
# tracked as plain blobs. The thresholds below are conservative; promote a
# new pattern to LFS when the asset class crosses ~10 MB cumulative.
#
# Verify on first clone:
#     git lfs install                # one-time, per machine
#     git clone <repo>               # downloads pointers + LFS objects
#     git lfs ls-files               # confirms LFS-tracked files
#
# Adding a new file under a tracked path: just `git add` and commit; LFS
# replaces with a pointer transparently.

# Formal usability-test stimuli — videos and screen recordings can be tens
# of MB per round. Patterns kept narrow so casual gifs in tests/ stay as
# plain blobs.
validation/listening-tests/**/*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
validation/listening-tests/**/*.mov filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Canonical Figma source files for the demo pipeline (.fig binary). Routinely
# > 5 MB. Stored as plain blobs only when below threshold; LFS is the default.
tools/demos/sources/**/*.fig filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Future: review-checkpoint kits and any large screenshot bundles may also
# want LFS once they grow past ~10 MB cumulative. Add tracking patterns here.

# pnpm lockfile auto-regenerate on conflict.
#
# When concurrent feature branches each add deps (e.g. section workspaces
# under sections/<Name>/), they all touch pnpm-lock.yaml. The first PR to
# merge sets the lockfile shape; subsequent PRs conflict on overlapping
# YAML line ranges even when their package.json deltas are independent.
#
# The 'pnpm-lockfile' driver (registered by scripts/install-merge-driver.sh,
# invoked by the root package.json `prepare` script on every `pnpm install`)
# resolves the conflict by re-running `pnpm install --lockfile-only` — the
# regenerated lockfile reflects all merged package.json files at once.
pnpm-lock.yaml merge=pnpm-lockfile
